# Git

Canonical for this repo **and** for `mcp-execution-engine` / `mcp-gateway`. Those repos point here — do not fork a second copy of these habits.

## When

- Do **not** commit, push, or open a PR unless the human asks.
- Working-tree **inspection** (branch, dirty files) is fine. Do not stash, checkout, create, rename, or push branches unless asked.
- **Exception:** [round-trip-branch-prep](../skills/round-trip-branch-prep/SKILL.md) at exploration GO (checkout / pull / safe-delete merged local branches / create `eip-NNNN`); [round-trip-close](../skills/round-trip-close/SKILL.md) at merge GO (merge PRs, delete remote `eip-NNNN`, checkout / pull default, safe-delete local). Those skills are the ask. Do not run them outside those phases.
- A **push** ask includes the [pre-push CI](#before-a-push) loop: fix failures and commit those fixes without a second ask, then push.

## Scope

Default: a commit / push / PR ask is **only the repo the work is in**. Do not inspect, commit, or push a sibling “because the three exist.” Independent sessions (engine-only, gateway-only, website-only) stay in that checkout — the others may be on a different branch or a dirty tree that is not this task.

**Round-trip only:** when this session is running [round-trip-protocol-change](../skills/round-trip-protocol-change/SKILL.md) (shared `eip-NNNN` branch is the extra signal), a commit / push / open-PRs ask means the three siblings that are dirty or ahead **on that work**. Skip a clean tree. Do not open a second PR. Do not touch a sibling that is on another branch or unrelated dirty state.

## Where git runs

This Cursor window is a **multi-root** workspace. The default shell cwd is often `ethereumjs-monorepo`, not these three repos.

Every git command for FYP (inspect included: `status`, `diff`, `log`) must:

1. Set `working_directory` to **that** repo’s root — `…/feelyourprotocol/website`, `…/mcp-execution-engine`, or `…/mcp-gateway`. Never the monorepo, never `feelyourprotocol/` as a parent folder.
2. Use `required_permissions: ["all"]`. The sandbox cannot open the monorepo’s cursorignored `packages/ethereum-tests` submodule; `git status` there dies with `error opening 'packages/ethereum-tests/.git': Operation not permitted`.

If you see `packages/ethereum-tests` in a git error, you are **not** in a FYP repo. Do not `cd` into that submodule or “fix” it. Re-run with the two bullets above.

When a round-trip inspects more than one sibling, those inspects may run in parallel **only** when both bullets are set. Anything that touches remotes or signing stays **strictly sequential** ([below](#sequential-workflow-fingerprint)).

## Sequential workflow (fingerprint)

Commits, **pushes**, **pulls** (`git pull` / `git fetch` + merge), and **PR opens** (`gh pr create`) must run **one shell command at a time**. Wait until that command **exits 0** before starting the next. The human approves signed git with **Touch ID / fingerprint**; parallel ops race for one prompt and fail (`agent refused operation`, `Permission denied (publickey)`).

**Never** batch parallel tool calls that include commit, push, pull, or `gh pr create` — even across different repos.

### One repo

Typical order when asked to ship:

1. Pre-push CI ([below](#before-a-push)) — may run local gates; not signed.
2. `git commit` → **wait success**
3. `git push` → **wait success**
4. `gh pr view` / `gh pr create` (if asked) → **wait success**
5. `gh pr edit` (if updating body) → **wait success**

If a **pull** is needed first: one `git pull` (or fetch + merge) → **wait success**, then continue.

### Several in-scope repos (round-trip or coordinated branch)

Same rule: **finish the full step for repo A before repo B** — do not push three branches in parallel or open three PRs in parallel.

Suggested dependency order when all three move together: **mcp-execution-engine → mcp-gateway → website** (engine probe, then gateway, then docs).

Per repo, in turn:

1. CI for that repo (if pushing)
2. Commit → wait success
3. Push → wait success
4. PR lookup / create → wait success

Then the next repo.

### Signing

Commits **and** pushes are signed (SSH / Touch ID). The human confirms with a fingerprint; that can take several seconds. Wait for the prompt — do not fire the next signed op until the previous one completed.

Do not retry in a tight loop, skip signing (`--no-gpg-sign` / `--no-verify`), or abandon because the signer has not answered yet. One failed push is not a reason to parallel-retry the other repos.

## Before a push

For **each repo that will be pushed**, run that repo’s local CI (the cheap gates GitHub runs). Do this **before** the push, after the work is committed or about to be.

| Repo | Local CI |
| --- | --- |
| `website` | `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run type-check` · `npm run test:unit:ci` |
| `mcp-execution-engine` | `npm run typecheck` · `npm run test:ci` · `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run build` |
| `mcp-gateway` | `npm run typecheck` · `npm run test:ci` · `npm run lf:ci` · `npm run build` |

Skip Cypress / `test:e2e` unless the change is Cypress or a user-visible flow that job already covers.

If a gate fails: fix it, re-run until green, **commit** the fix (same [commit-message](#commit-messages) habits) without waiting for another “please commit.” Then push. Do not push red.

Same `working_directory` + `required_permissions: ["all"]` as other git. CI is not a signed git op — if several in-scope repos need it, they may run in parallel.

## After a push

A push does not say whether a PR exists. For **each repo you just pushed** (one repo at a time, after that push succeeded), look it up (`gh pr view <branch>`).

- **PR exists:** update **that** PR’s body to match the work now on the branch. Do not open a second PR. On a **round-trip** PR, keep the [arch shape](#pull-requests), refresh **Round-trip** and **So far**, and do not retitle. Do not turn the body into a changelog of the last commit.
- **No PR:** say so. Open one only if the human asked to open PRs — **`gh pr create` immediately after that repo’s push**, wait success, then move to the next repo.

Report the PR URL (or “no PR”) after each repo’s push+PR step. `gh pr edit` is not a signed git op — still run it **sequentially**, with `working_directory` + `required_permissions: ["all"]`.

## Commit messages

One line. Name the artifact **and** what changed — a bare `EIP-8038 MCP` is too thin. No conventional-commit prefix (`feat:`, `fix:`), no body, no invented PR numbers (`#52` is added on merge).

Normally **7–10 words**. Compact, not a paragraph.

```
EIP-7843 SLOTNUM exploration and cover
EIP-8038 MCP module and run_bytecode message-call
EIP-8038 Bro & Bruh comic
Twitter reply voice refinement
```

- EIP work: `EIP-NNNN` + artifact + a short change clue (what this commit actually landed).
- Otherwise Title Case at the same length (`Launch Strategy draft`, `CI job budget`).
- If unsure, read `git log --oneline` on that repo and add one more specific noun than a generic artifact label.

## Pull requests

Same shortness as commit subjects. No conventional-commit prefix. No template checklist.

**Round-trip** (this session is running the round-trip skill; shared `eip-NNNN` branch is the extra signal): every repo’s PR is the **bigger-context** PR. Title is just the EIP id (`EIP-8038`) — including engine/gateway, even if the first commits are only supporting (git rules, scaffolding). That PR will accumulate the exploration / MCP work. Do not name it after the first commit.

Description: the **arch**, not the latest commit. Same shape, **not** the same text — each repo writes its own slice.

- **website** — the exploration (widget, cover, home, mcp-docs twin).
- **mcp-execution-engine** — the engine module / verbs / catalog.
- **mcp-gateway** — transport: exposing those verbs, no per-EIP tool.

Within that slice:

- **What** — one or two lines: this repo’s part of the EIP.
- **Round-trip** — where this repo sits (exploration vs MCP vs later); mark done vs open.
- **So far** — what shipped **here**; what still lands on this PR. After later pushes, this is the line that moves.

Do **not** copy the website body onto engine/gateway. Do **not** narrate supporting commits (`git.mdc`, templates). Those are not the PR.

Engine and gateway bodies **link** the website GitHub PR (the exploration). Do not paste its description.

- After that main PR: follow-ups add the artifact (`EIP-8038 Comic + Video`) and a short artifact-specific body.
- Outside a round-trip: two-to-five-word title (`Git rules`); short structured body is fine.
